WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.660
Which leaf clues would you trust in summer and fall?

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.460
In thirty minutes, you can help your child sort fallen leaves and test which clues

3
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.460
hold up.

4
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:16.633
Your child practices looking closely and sorting by one rule at a time.

5
00:00:17.773 --> 00:00:20.813
They also practice asking a question and checking it.

6
00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:26.513
Instead of memorizing tree names, they discover which leaf clues stay steady all

7
00:00:26.513 --> 00:00:27.513
year.

8
00:00:27.817 --> 00:00:33.237
Grab a bag or basket, two hoops or string loops, thin paper, and a peeled crayon.

9
00:00:34.477 --> 00:00:38.437
Take a ten to fifteen minute walk to collect eight to twelve different fallen

10
00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:42.417
leaves. Printed leaf photos work if you stay indoors.

11
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.510
Stay with your child outdoors and keep them in sight.

12
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.430
Keep them within arm’s reach near roads, parking lots, and water.

13
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.870
Collect only fallen leaves where permitted.

14
00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:00.010
Leave unfamiliar leaves and leaves with three leaflets on the ground.

15
00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.390
Even dead or fallen poison ivy can cause a rash.

16
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.770
Never taste any plant part or mushroom.

17
00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.590
Wash hands after collecting.

18
00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:15.810
Keep crayon pieces and small finds away from children under four.

19
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:18.547
Spread out the leaves you checked.

20
00:01:19.847 --> 00:01:21.827
Look at their edges, veins, and tips.

21
00:01:23.127 --> 00:01:25.187
Keep spiny leaves for the adult to handle.

22
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.627
Let your child sort the leaves into two piles.

23
00:01:29.887 --> 00:01:31.307
Then ask, "What was your rule?"

24
00:01:31.827 --> 00:01:32.827
Write down their words.

25
00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:37.203
Next, sort by one feature at a time using the two hoops.

26
00:01:38.463 --> 00:01:43.863
Try one leaf together first: needle or flat, smooth edge or toothed, lobes or no

27
00:01:43.863 --> 00:01:48.763
lobes. Before each round, ask, "Which hoop do you think will hold more?"

28
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:53.487
A bright red leaf makes color tempting as a sorting rule.

29
00:01:54.887 --> 00:01:57.147
Imagine using that same rule again in summer.

30
00:01:58.387 --> 00:02:00.787
Would leaves from the same tree land in the same pile?

31
00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.970
A red maple leaf can be green in summer.

32
00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.670
In fall, it can be red, orange, or yellow.

33
00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.150
Ask your child, "What color was this leaf back in July?"

34
00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:22.230
If you can find two differently colored leaves from the same tree, compare their

35
00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.230
shapes and edges.

36
00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:28.003
Slide a leaf under thin paper with the veins facing up.

37
00:02:29.003 --> 00:02:30.823
Rub with the side of a peeled crayon.

38
00:02:32.023 --> 00:02:33.543
The veins and edges show up.

39
00:02:34.703 --> 00:02:37.443
For older children, try a yes-or-no key together.

40
00:02:38.843 --> 00:02:40.223
Ask whether the leaf is a needle.

41
00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:43.823
If it is, the example key leads to pine.

42
00:02:44.917 --> 00:02:47.917
A key only covers the leaves you built it for.

43
00:02:49.177 --> 00:02:51.457
Help your child write questions for your own set.

44
00:02:52.697 --> 00:02:56.037
Give someone a leaf from that set and ask them to try the key.

45
00:02:57.417 --> 00:02:59.397
Rewrite any question that trips them up.

46
00:03:00.397 --> 00:03:03.337
Your child practices choosing clues that stay steady.
